---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp-lua/img/bp-bouncing-logo-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const PrintText = `love.graphics.print("AM", x + 60, y + 32)`;

const Move = `x = x + dx * dt
y = y + dy * dt`;

const BounceLeft = `if x < 0 then
    x = 0
    dx = -dx
end`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp-lua/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Bonus Project: Bouncing Logo</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Is</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The screensaver everybody has watched
            waiting for something to happen.</b> A logo drifts across a black
            screen, hits an edge, and goes the other way. You will build it,
            and then you will find out how long you have to watch before it
            hits a corner.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>This is extra credit and it is optional.</b> Nothing later in the
            course depends on it. It exists because you finished early, or
            because you want to build something you can show somebody.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="before">
          <h2>Before You Start</h2>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">You need everything through session 9.</b>{" "}
            Variables, <CodeSnippet code="love.update(dt)" lang="lua" inline />,
            and an <CodeSnippet code="if" lang="lua" inline /> that checks an
            edge. That is all &mdash; this is the smallest project on the list
            and it is the one you can finish in twenty minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>One thing here is new.</b> Drawing text on the screen:
          </p>

          <Code code={PrintText} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet code="love.graphics.print" lang="lua" inline /> takes
            the text, then an x and a y. That is the whole thing. Put your own
            initials in it &mdash; it is your logo.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Copy <CodeSnippet code="template-game" lang="txt" inline /> and
            rename it <CodeSnippet code="bp-bouncing-logo" lang="txt" inline />.
            No new files, no libraries, nothing to install.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="tasks">
          <h2>Build It</h2>

          <p>
            Four stages. <b class="text-sn-theme">Run it after every one.</b> A
            stage that works is worth more than three stages you cannot test.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">1. A rectangle that sits there</h3>

          <p>
            Variables for <CodeSnippet code="x" lang="lua" inline />,{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="y" lang="lua" inline />,{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="w" lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="h" lang="lua" inline />, and one{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="love.graphics.rectangle" lang="lua" inline /> that
            uses all four. Nothing moves yet.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            Give it a color you like. This is on your screen for the rest of the
            project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">2. Make it drift</h3>

          <p>
            Two more variables &mdash; call them{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="dx" lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="dy" lang="lua" inline /> &mdash; for{" "}
            <b>how far it moves each second in each direction</b>. Then, in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="love.update" lang="lua" inline />:
          </p>

          <Code code={Move} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            It will slide off the screen and never come back. That is correct
            for stage 2.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-4">3. Bounce off all four edges</h3>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Four separate conditions, one per edge.</b>{" "}
            The left one looks like this:
          </p>

          <Code code={BounceLeft} lang="lua" frame="code" showLineNumbers={false} />

          <p>
            <b>The minus sign is the whole trick.</b> Going left becomes going
            right. And the <CodeSnippet code="x = 0" lang="lua" inline /> is not
            decoration &mdash; without it the logo can end up slightly past the
            edge, test true again next frame, and flip back. Leave it out once
            on purpose and watch what happens.
          </p>

          <div class="alert alert-info">
            <b>The right edge is not <code>x &gt; 800</code>.</b> The rectangle
            is drawn from its left corner, so the right edge is at{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="x + w" lang="lua" inline />. Get this wrong and
            the logo disappears most of the way off screen before it turns
            around, which is a very easy bug to see and a very easy one to miss.
          </div>

          <h3 class="mt-4">4. Put your initials on it</h3>

          <p>
            Draw the text <b>after</b> the rectangle so it lands on top, and
            offset it by the same{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="x" lang="lua" inline /> and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet code="y" lang="lua" inline /> so it travels with the
            box rather than staying where you put it.
          </p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            That last part is session 3's idea again: one variable moving
            several things at once.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="sample-output">
          <h2>What Success Looks Like</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image
              src={TargetImage}
              alt="A black 800 by 600 window with a cyan rectangle labeled A M touching the right edge, low on the screen"
              width={800}
              class="img-fluid rounded"
            />
          </div>

          <p>
            The logo is caught at the right edge, the frame before it turns
            around. It travels forever, it never leaves the window, and{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">the initials go with it.</b>
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <section id="extend">
          <h2>Then Make It Yours</h2>

          <ul>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Change color on every bounce.</b> Three variables for red,
              green and blue, and a new set of values each time an edge is hit.{" "}
              <CodeSnippet code="math.random()" lang="lua" inline /> with no
              arguments gives you a number between 0 and 1, which is exactly the
              scale <CodeSnippet code="setColor" lang="lua" inline /> wants.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Count the corners.</b> A corner hit is both an x edge and a y
              edge in the same frame. Count them and draw the count.{" "}
              <b class="text-sn-theme">Then go and do something else for ten
              minutes and see what the number says</b> &mdash; if it is climbing
              fast, your two speeds divide into each other too neatly.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Speed up over time.</b> A tiny amount every second. Then find
              the speed at which it starts passing through walls, and work out
              why that happens. This is a real bug in real games and it has a
              name worth knowing.
            </li>
            <li class="mb-2">
              <b>Two logos.</b> Then five. Notice how much copying and pasting
              that takes &mdash; sessions 18 and 21 are both about the fix.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Submit whatever you have.</b> A logo bouncing off two edges still
            earns credit, and so does a finished one with a bug you can
            describe.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Project Complete
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
